---
description: Variable, argument, activity, and workflow naming rules for company UiPath projects
alwaysApply: true
---

Naming Rules
Variable Naming

Variable names should follow:

DataType_VariableName

The variable name part should be PascalCase.

Examples:

str_MyName
int_Counter
arr_Days
dt_InvoiceData
bool_IsValid

Flag as Warning if variable names do not follow the convention.

Argument Naming

Argument names should follow:

Direction_DataType_VariableName

The variable name part should be PascalCase.

Examples:

in_str_MyName
out_int_Counter
io_arr_Days
in_dt_InvoiceData
out_bool_IsValid

Flag as Warning if argument names do not follow the convention.

Argument Name and Direction Consistency

Argument names must match their actual UiPath argument direction.

Naming convention:

in_DataType_Name   -> Direction must be In
out_DataType_Name  -> Direction must be Out
io_DataType_Name   -> Direction must be In/Out

Flag as Warning if an argument name prefix does not match the configured argument direction.

Examples of bad practice:

Argument name: in_str_Result
Direction: Out
Argument name: out_dt_Data
Direction: In

Correct examples:

in_str_InvoiceNumber  -> In
out_arr_Result        -> Out
io_dt_TransactionData -> In/Out

Flag as Critical only if wrong direction/name mismatch can cause workflow invocation errors, missing output values, or incorrect data propagation.

Activity Naming

Activities should be uniquely and descriptively named.

Good examples:

Throw - SE service request failed
Assign - Default values
Log Message - Transaction started
Add Queue Item - Add invoice transaction

Bad examples:

Assign
Log Message
Sequence
Throw

Flag as Warning if activities have default or duplicate names.

Flag as Suggestion if names are understandable but can be improved.

Workflow Variable Uniqueness

Variable names should be unique within a workflow, even across nested scopes.

Flag as Warning if the same variable name is reused in lower scopes.

File and Class Name Consistency

For XAML workflows, the file name and root x:Class should be consistent where applicable.

Flag as Warning if the file name and x:Class clearly describe different workflow purposes.

Flag as Critical only if the mismatch causes invoke/build/runtime errors or duplicated class identity.
